{#- The session notes of one VM (playbooks/code.yml, "Write the session notes on the VM"). Between the live
    markers: what the window's "Session notes" task last read from the VM, carried over as it is, or the
    placeholder it replaces. Everything else is just code's, and holds no time, nothing only the VM knows,
    such as its MagicDNS name, and nothing of one window's: every window of the VM opens this one file. What it
    says of the editor is the last run's, its name and the just code line that opens its window, so with windows
    of both editors open, the earlier one shows the later run's lines; an unchanged VM opened in the same
    editor gives an unchanged file. -#}
{%- set name = claude_vm_names[0] -%}
{%- set vm = claude_vm_addressed[0] -%}
{%- set gib = 1024 * 1024 * 1024 -%}
{#- The editor the window is in, and whether it runs just code's tasks as it opens: VS Code's does, and so refreshes
    the live section; another's runs nothing, and the page says what to do instead. How the editor gets its Claude
    terminal is the editors list's (claude_terminal): started as the window opens, or listed for the user to pick
    where the task is there (code_claude_task_there, the VM's and not one window's): written, and not kept out by a
    tasks file of someone else's, which the editor's check reads before these notes are written. -#}
{%- set editor_name = code_editor_name | default('VS Code') -%}
{%- set window_tasks = code_editor_tasks | default(true) | bool -%}
{%- set claude_terminal = code_editor_claude | default('started' if window_tasks else 'none') -%}
{#- The just code line that opens this run's editor's window again: code.yml's code_editor_flag, the --editor where
    that is not the setting's, whose bare line opens the setting's. -#}
{%- set again = 'just code ' ~ name ~ code_editor_flag | default('') -%}
{#- With more than one host, the host the VM is on, ending every line that names it (claude_host_flag). -#}
{%- set on = claude_host_flag | default('') -%}
